pr_comments:
@SeppoTakalo There are perhaps other areas where thread safety could be improved, this PR addresses the ones I've identified and seemed quite obvious. Regarding your points:

> We have also shared arrays in context
	struct coap_pending pendings[];
	struct coap_reply replies[];
	
I had an impression those are used exclusively by the LwM2M thread, but apparently that's not the case. It looks we can reuse the LwM2M context lock to protect access to the arrays, I will update the PR.

> Then lwm2m_engine.c have some global arrays that might be modified by API call from external thread
static struct service_node service_node_data[];
static sys_slist_t engine_service_list;
static struct zsock_pollfd sock_fds[];
static struct lwm2m_ctx *sock_ctx[];
static int sock_nfds;

Those need closer investigation whether they actually need additional protection. If I understand correctly those would only be modified when the client starts/stops, and those called have some locking implemented. Haven't analized though  closely how much sense that mechanism has, at least I didn't encounter any issue here.

> And lwm2m_message_handling.c also has some global arrays for messages
static struct lwm2m_message messages[];
static struct lwm2m_block_context block1_contexts[];

I've introduced a global lock for message allocation, but it's a good point, we should probably extend it for block context management as well. I'll update that commit with a global lock instead, and reuse it for block context.

> (And soon we have a danger of deadlock)

And that's what I'm mostly concerned about and thus want to keep those changes simple. We already have registry lock, Rd client lock, introducing extra mutexes covering complex operations will yield a really high risk of introducing deadlocks.
